# Pictures, rich content and embedded pages

A picture can help someone recognise a room or a device. It must not be the only place where a state is shown or an action is offered. Treat a household drawing, a camera image and an external website as three different kinds of content. Each has its own evidence and privacy needs.

These examples are local previews with invented household values. They do not call Home Assistant. Open an example to see it at full width or read its HTML.

<h2 id="pictures">Picture card families</h2>

| Native family | Purpose | Required equivalent |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Picture | An image, with a navigation or action if one is set | A useful name for the destination or action. Alternative text only when the image adds meaning. |
| Picture entity | An image tied to an entity, or a supported camera view | The entity name, its current state, and the time or source of the image when it matters |
| Picture glance | An image with several entity states and actions on top | The same named states and actions, in reading order, reachable by keyboard |
| Picture elements | State labels, badges, icons, images, conditional elements and action buttons placed on an image | A list in normal reading order with the same information and actions |

A decorative drawing of a room is not a live view, and a retained camera image is not current footage. For image age and stream failures, see [Cameras, streams and snapshots](cameras.html#ha-ctrl-015-states). If the artwork is missing, keep the names, states and actions. Do not crop out a hazard or an access detail that matters.

