<h2 id="overlays">Overlay elements and action semantics</h2>

The state label, state icon and state badge elements show real entity information. A decorative icon has no state. An image element needs a stated source and suitable alternative text. A conditional element shows its sub-elements only for the entity states you list. Hidden elements must not leave invisible targets for keyboard focus. Leave room between overlays so names, values and units can grow.

The picture elements editor and documentation call the button element `action-button`. Home Assistant maps it to its `service-button` element, so saved dashboards that use that name keep working. Use the current name when you write for people.

Tap, hold and double-tap are ways to activate something. They do not describe the action. Each action keeps its own meaning: `more-info`, `toggle`, `perform-action`, `navigate`, `url`, `assist` and `none`. If an essential operation sits behind hold or double-tap, also give it a named ordinary control. For an action with consequences, use the native `confirmation` option so the person is asked first. A picture must never run an action just because it loaded.

<h2 id="rich-content">Markdown and authored content</h2>

Use Markdown to explain something useful, not to build a second dashboard inside the first. Keep headings, lists, useful links and a written explanation of each state. A generated template must cope with a missing value and with escaping, using the native renderer. Treat text supplied by a device as data. Do not run it as HTML or as instructions. If a dynamic sentence vanishes after an error, show a steady error message in its place.

Do not use CSS colour or position to make ordinary text look like a control. Use the native actions, or a real link or button in a component civica owns. Make clear whether a destination is inside Home Assistant or outside it. The [States, requests and errors](../content/writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html) rules apply to generated text too.

