<h2 id="acceptance">Acceptance checks</h2>

Test overlays with long translations at 320px and with zoom, with broken images, with an unavailable entity and with a blocked frame. Reach every essential action without coordinates, holding or double-tapping. Turn images off and check that the names and states still make sense. Check that keyboard focus cannot enter a hidden conditional element. Confirm that opening the page sends no device request and fetches no external media before the chosen privacy boundary.

<h2 id="evidence">Sources and evidence</h2>

Checked on 17 November 2026 against the Home Assistant documentation for [Picture elements](https://www.home-assistant.io/dashboards/picture-elements/), [Picture entity](https://www.home-assistant.io/dashboards/picture-entity/), [Webpage](https://www.home-assistant.io/dashboards/iframe/), [Markdown](https://www.home-assistant.io/dashboards/markdown/) and [dashboard actions](https://www.home-assistant.io/dashboards/actions/), and the Home Assistant frontend source at commit `b1164739c3a853a28ba1c425415e96a17eb6286e` for the `action-button` mapping.

This is a design specification. It does not replace the native components. The examples use invented records and local feedback. Mapping a native surface to this page names who owns the design. It does not show that every integration, browser or device has been tested. Keep native state formatting, translations, permissions and event handling. A theme can change how something looks. It cannot supply behaviour the application lacks.

The [interface coverage inventory](../reference/ha-interface-coverage.json) lists the native surfaces and their mappings. Recheck native behaviour after a frontend change. Record real tests using [Native task accessibility and evidence](native-task-accessibility.html).

